1
00:00:09,977 --> 00:00:11,563
I'd like you to take
a look at this gun.

2
00:00:12,564 --> 00:00:14,818
It's handcrafted to my
specifications.

3
00:00:15,309 --> 00:00:16,827
Balance is excellent.

4
00:00:17,083 --> 00:00:19,207
The trigger responds to
a pressure of 1 ounce.

5
00:00:20,407 --> 00:00:21,144
I'm...

6
00:00:21,519 --> 00:00:23,340
...quite familiar with
the piece.

7
00:00:23,586 --> 00:00:25,221
Perhaps we could
talk about this?

8
00:00:25,341 --> 00:00:27,269
I rarely draw it unless
I mean to use it.

9
00:00:27,747 --> 00:00:29,366
Care for a demonstration?

10
00:00:33,326 --> 00:00:34,408
SCOTT:
Freeze program.

11
00:00:40,857 --> 00:00:42,566
Was she everything
I promised, sir?

12
00:00:42,862 --> 00:00:43,809
And more...

13
00:00:44,808 --> 00:00:47,687
I've never seen a holographic
program this detailed before.

14
00:00:47,751 --> 00:00:49,686
I swear I can almost
smell the gun oil.

15
00:00:49,961 --> 00:00:52,708
Aye. Well she's still in the experimental
stage, but once we get the bugs out...

16
00:00:52,828 --> 00:00:54,053
[INTERCOM WHISTLES]

17
00:00:54,302 --> 00:00:56,078
SPOCK:
Bridge to Captain Kirk...

18
00:00:56,198 --> 00:00:56,994
Kirk here.

19
00:00:57,114 --> 00:01:00,501
SPOCK:
We've arrived at the first of the power
stations that have gone offline, captain.

20
00:01:00,545 --> 00:01:01,451
I'll be right there.

21
00:01:02,492 --> 00:01:03,343
All right...

22
00:01:03,463 --> 00:01:04,518
Playtime's over...

23
00:01:04,638 --> 00:01:05,516
Let's get back to work.

24
00:01:16,639 --> 00:01:17,693
KIRK:
Report, Spock.

25
00:01:18,688 --> 00:01:20,695
Starfleet's communique
was accurate.

26
00:01:20,722 --> 00:01:23,627
All 3 power stations in this
system are completely drained.

27
00:01:23,848 --> 00:01:25,301
Any idea what
caused it?

28
00:01:25,421 --> 00:01:27,884
Aye. Likely the Klingons
stealing fuel cells again.

29
00:01:28,004 --> 00:01:30,550
There is no evidence to support
that theory, Mr. Scott.

30
00:01:30,670 --> 00:01:31,956
That's because it
wasn't a theory.

31
00:01:32,076 --> 00:01:33,492
It was a joke.

32
00:01:35,222 --> 00:01:37,441
The power stations were
anchored in this system...

33
00:01:37,478 --> 00:01:40,614
...with the purpose of providing energy
and fuel for passing Federation ships.

34
00:01:41,073 --> 00:01:42,978
And what would cause a
failure on that scale?

35
00:01:43,330 --> 00:01:44,125
Unknown.

36
00:01:44,441 --> 00:01:45,818
If theft was intended...

37
00:01:45,938 --> 00:01:48,641
...it would be far more efficient to
steal the stations themselves...

38
00:01:48,761 --> 00:01:50,778
...as opposed to siphoning
them one-by-one.

39
00:01:50,898 --> 00:01:51,923
SULU:
Captain...

40
00:01:52,043 --> 00:01:54,350
I'm registering a minor
drain on engine power.

41
00:01:57,104 --> 00:01:57,899
Scotty?

42
00:01:58,019 --> 00:01:58,850
Checking.

43
00:01:59,867 --> 00:02:00,595
Aye.

44
00:02:00,965 --> 00:02:02,191
Slight,
but noticeable.

45
00:02:02,311 --> 00:02:03,463
Any danger?

46
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:05,069
Not yet, sir. But I don't like it.

47
00:02:05,091 --> 00:02:06,490
I can't even tell you
what's causing it.

48
00:02:07,646 --> 00:02:09,056
All right...

49
00:02:09,176 --> 00:02:10,051
Spock.

50
00:02:11,149 --> 00:02:12,501
Scan the station...

51
00:02:13,007 --> 00:02:14,648
...for any clues to the
power loss.

52
00:02:14,899 --> 00:02:15,699
CHEKOV:
Captain...

53
00:02:15,819 --> 00:02:19,171
I'm reading something 320,000
kilometers off the starboard bow.

54
00:02:20,015 --> 00:02:21,032
Put it on screen.

55
00:02:25,762 --> 00:02:26,987
What the devil...

56
00:02:30,710 --> 00:02:31,519
Spock?

57
00:02:31,820 --> 00:02:32,618
Unknown.

58
00:02:32,738 --> 00:02:35,129
Sulu, bring us around.
Scotty, shields up.

59
00:02:35,249 --> 00:02:37,866
Shields not responding.
Power drain has accelerated.

60
00:02:38,610 --> 00:02:41,407
The source of the drain is the
anomaly directly in front of us.

61
00:02:42,073 --> 00:02:43,492
Can we do anything
about it?

62
00:02:43,612 --> 00:02:44,374
Unknown.

63
00:02:45,234 --> 00:02:48,500
Ship's systems, including life
support, at 54 percent and falling.

64
00:02:49,627 --> 00:02:51,592
What is it? A machine?
An organism?

65
00:02:51,787 --> 00:02:52,632
Unknown.

66
00:02:52,657 --> 00:02:55,191
That's an awful lot of unknowns,
Mr. Spock. I need answers.

67
00:02:55,311 --> 00:02:56,326
Go to red alert.

68
00:02:56,535 --> 00:02:59,153
Sulu, put some distance between
us... Back away...

69
00:03:00,013 --> 00:03:01,520
Engines not responding, captain.

70
00:03:01,826 --> 00:03:02,612
I can't seem to...

71
00:03:02,649 --> 00:03:04,360
Warp drive and impulse
engines are offline.

72
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:05,506
Uhura, anything?

73
00:03:05,543 --> 00:03:06,367
Nothing, sir.

74
00:03:06,394 --> 00:03:08,671
I've tried friendship messages on
all known hailing frequencies!

75
00:03:08,995 --> 00:03:11,573
Ship's power now at 29 percent!
She's fading fast!

76
00:03:11,693 --> 00:03:12,787
Not without a fight.

77
00:03:12,907 --> 00:03:13,596
Ready phasers...

78
00:03:13,716 --> 00:03:15,231
We don't have enough
power for phasers, sir.

79
00:03:15,254 --> 00:03:17,392
I had to divert everything we
have to life support.

80
00:03:17,420 --> 00:03:18,453
Photon torpedos then.

81
00:03:18,490 --> 00:03:21,117
Aye... we might be able to get off
one shot, but there's no telling if...

82
00:03:21,148 --> 00:03:23,139
Lock photon torpedo on it.
Dead center.

83
00:03:23,494 --> 00:03:24,493
Torpedo locked.

84
00:03:24,749 --> 00:03:25,482
Fire!

85
00:03:28,190 --> 00:03:29,137
20 seconds to impact.

86
00:03:29,588 --> 00:03:31,046
Power levels at 14 percent.

87
00:03:31,359 --> 00:03:34,836
Captain, I'm detecting two life forms
in the center of the anomaly.

88
00:03:35,251 --> 00:03:36,116
Life forms?

89
00:03:36,236 --> 00:03:36,871
McCOY:
Jim...

90
00:03:36,905 --> 00:03:38,818
SPOCK:
Readings are very faint but definite.

91
00:03:38,873 --> 00:03:40,138
McCOY:
Jim, you don't know
anything about them.

92
00:03:40,168 --> 00:03:41,107
They could need
our help.

93
00:03:41,152 --> 00:03:42,032
Scotty. Transporters.

94
00:03:42,084 --> 00:03:44,134
No way, sir! Power levels at 9 percent!

95
00:03:44,363 --> 00:03:45,428
CHEKOV:
Impact in 5 seconds.

96
00:03:45,673 --> 00:03:46,109
McCOY:
Jim!

97
00:03:46,154 --> 00:03:46,812
We're out of options!

98
00:03:46,932 --> 00:03:47,500
MCCOY:
But you don't know if...

99
00:03:47,620 --> 00:03:48,562
Impact!

100
00:03:57,163 --> 00:03:58,095
Report.

101
00:03:58,317 --> 00:03:59,582
SCOTT:
Power levels at 12 percent.

102
00:04:00,434 --> 00:04:01,300
19 percent.

103
00:04:01,625 --> 00:04:02,742
Increasing steadily.

104
00:04:02,862 --> 00:04:05,302
All decks reporting in.
No casualties.

105
00:04:05,422 --> 00:04:08,420
Our torpedo broke off small
pieces of the anomaly.

106
00:04:08,540 --> 00:04:12,352
The bulk is still intact,
but it is no longer a viable threat.

107
00:04:14,655 --> 00:04:17,003
I detect no life forms.

108
00:04:22,831 --> 00:04:24,389
Cancel red alert.

109
00:04:27,099 --> 00:04:28,882
I'm sorry, Bones.

110
00:04:29,537 --> 00:04:30,763
But it couldn't be helped.

111
00:04:30,791 --> 00:04:31,771
I know, Jim.

112
00:04:32,086 --> 00:04:35,323
It's just hard to see the taking of any life,
even if it's to save our own.

113
00:04:35,690 --> 00:04:36,661
SCOTT:
24 percent.

114
00:04:36,994 --> 00:04:37,900
31 percent.

115
00:04:38,168 --> 00:04:39,019
Still climbing.

116
00:04:39,139 --> 00:04:41,470
Captain, I'm detecting an
energy buildup.

117
00:04:44,853 --> 00:04:47,239
SPOCK:
Focused within the Enterprise.

118
00:04:48,802 --> 00:04:50,495
Can you identify an
exact location?

119
00:04:51,447 --> 00:04:52,576
The bridge.

120
00:05:23,216 --> 00:05:24,261
Space...

121
00:05:24,576 --> 00:05:26,185
The final frontier.

122
00:05:34,682 --> 00:05:37,836
These are the voyages of
the Starship Enterprise.

123
00:05:38,585 --> 00:05:39,981
Its continuing mission...

124
00:05:40,508 --> 00:05:42,904
To explore strange new worlds...

125
00:05:43,579 --> 00:05:46,770
To seek out new life
and new civilizations.

126
00:05:47,482 --> 00:05:50,451
To boldly go
where no man has gone before.

127
00:06:30,242 --> 00:06:33,108
KIRK (V.O.):
Captain's log, stardate 6147.3.

128
00:06:33,228 --> 00:06:36,240
It's been nearly two years
since the alien called Apollo...

129
00:06:36,270 --> 00:06:39,346
...from Earth's ancient history tried to
enslave us on Pollux 4.

130
00:06:39,466 --> 00:06:42,771
Now, a much older Apollo has
reappeared. Only this time...

131
00:06:42,891 --> 00:06:44,548
...on board the Enterprise.

132
00:06:44,668 --> 00:06:47,064
Security to the bridge.
On the double.

133
00:06:48,249 --> 00:06:49,231
Apollo?

134
00:06:56,236 --> 00:06:58,697
A thousand years...

135
00:06:59,194 --> 00:07:00,932
in a day...

136
00:07:01,452 --> 00:07:03,551
Rest dearest.

137
00:07:03,671 --> 00:07:06,126
We continue because of you.

138
00:07:06,667 --> 00:07:08,655
You continue.

139
00:07:09,422 --> 00:07:11,183
I am pleased...

140
00:07:11,748 --> 00:07:14,683
The final image my eyes behold...

141
00:07:15,112 --> 00:07:16,964
...is that of your face.

142
00:07:17,084 --> 00:07:18,373
No...

143
00:07:18,712 --> 00:07:20,744
We're safe together.

144
00:07:21,986 --> 00:07:23,838
You can't go.

145
00:07:24,154 --> 00:07:25,734
I forbid it.

146
00:07:38,726 --> 00:07:40,194
Live on...

147
00:07:40,645 --> 00:07:42,452
...for us.

148
00:07:51,133 --> 00:07:52,352
Sleep...

149
00:07:52,849 --> 00:07:54,678
Dwell upon thine eyes.

150
00:07:55,807 --> 00:07:57,546
Peace in thy breast.

151
00:07:58,404 --> 00:08:00,526
Would I were sleep in peace...

152
00:08:00,887 --> 00:08:03,078
...so sweet to rest.

153
00:08:05,086 --> 00:08:06,689
Travel gently...

154
00:08:07,547 --> 00:08:09,196
...beloved Athena.

155
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:20,915
UHURA:
Where did she go?

156
00:08:27,103 --> 00:08:28,726
I don't know.

157
00:08:30,737 --> 00:08:33,101
Emptiness.

158
00:08:41,074 --> 00:08:43,862
Don't know what I can do for him.
But let's get him to sickbay.

159
00:08:43,982 --> 00:08:45,522
Sir?
You're not actually going to...

160
00:09:16,525 --> 00:09:19,256
Power's stabilized, sir. Shield
output is increasing but...

161
00:09:19,376 --> 00:09:20,138
But...?

162
00:09:20,258 --> 00:09:22,566
Well, pieces of whatever that
thing out there was, sir.

163
00:09:22,578 --> 00:09:23,919
They've hung to the ship's hull.

164
00:09:24,039 --> 00:09:27,021
SULU:
Captain, if that debris clogs the
intake manifolds...

165
00:09:27,141 --> 00:09:29,912
SCOTT:
I've sealed them, but we are stuck
here until we can clear that debris.

166
00:09:30,032 --> 00:09:33,681
SPOCK:
Readings indicate the material to
be innocuous once hardened.

167
00:09:33,801 --> 00:09:37,042
There is, however, an unknown corrosive
substance at their centers.

168
00:09:37,162 --> 00:09:38,718
Corrosive enough to eat
through the hull?

169
00:09:38,838 --> 00:09:40,106
Possibly...

170
00:09:41,088 --> 00:09:43,111
Suggestions, gentlemen?

171
00:09:43,231 --> 00:09:44,476
SCOTTY:
What a whaup i the raip...

172
00:09:44,596 --> 00:09:46,853
We could go out on the hull with
a couple hand phasers.

173
00:09:47,189 --> 00:09:49,246
Cut the residue from
the ship's exterior.

174
00:09:49,366 --> 00:09:51,372
SPOCK:
Interesting strategy, Mr. Sulu.

175
00:09:51,492 --> 00:09:53,844
By setting phasers to the proper
resonant frequency...

176
00:09:53,964 --> 00:09:57,359
...we should be able to easily scrape it
off the hull without damaging it.

177
00:09:57,479 --> 00:09:59,151
SCOTT:
Aye. Like sandpaper.

178
00:09:59,271 --> 00:10:01,602
But it's precision work.
We can't afford any bumps.

179
00:10:01,722 --> 00:10:03,219
I think Chekov can
handle that.

180
00:10:04,202 --> 00:10:06,849
Well, Mr. Sulu?
Up for a little walk outside?

181
00:10:06,969 --> 00:10:07,693
Absolutely.

182
00:10:07,813 --> 00:10:08,456
Very well...

183
00:10:08,576 --> 00:10:11,377
You and Simone from structural
engineering suit up and get it done.

184
00:10:11,497 --> 00:10:14,302
Mr. Scott will monitor
the EVA from here.

185
00:10:17,502 --> 00:10:19,308
You look...

186
00:10:19,341 --> 00:10:20,691
...concerned, Mr. Spock.

187
00:10:20,811 --> 00:10:21,783
I am, captain.

188
00:10:21,903 --> 00:10:23,420
I think Sulu knows
what he's doing.

189
00:10:23,457 --> 00:10:25,214
It is not Sulu that concerns me.

190
00:10:25,427 --> 00:10:26,509
It is Apollo.

191
00:10:26,915 --> 00:10:27,944
Yes.

192
00:10:28,884 --> 00:10:32,551
I don't like him here one bit but
I feel somewhat responsible for him...

193
00:10:32,597 --> 00:10:33,581
...being here.

194
00:10:33,618 --> 00:10:36,223
Curious he has aged so
rapidly in 2 years.

195
00:10:36,701 --> 00:10:37,950
KIRK:
Computer...

196
00:10:38,204 --> 00:10:40,644
Analyze subject's age on screen.

197
00:10:41,083 --> 00:10:42,135
COMPUTER (V.O.):
Working...

198
00:10:42,157 --> 00:10:44,874
Subject on screen's physiological age...

199
00:10:44,895 --> 00:10:46,797
...based on established norms...

200
00:10:46,848 --> 00:10:50,082
...between 38 and 41 Earth years.

201
00:10:50,304 --> 00:10:53,056
Now, analyze the age of the
subject currently in sickbay.

202
00:10:53,472 --> 00:10:54,686
COMPUTER (V.O.):
Working...

203
00:10:54,806 --> 00:10:57,787
Subject in sickbay's physiological age...

204
00:10:57,810 --> 00:10:59,371
...based on established norms...

205
00:10:59,394 --> 00:11:02,296
...between 77 and 82 Earth years.

206
00:11:03,129 --> 00:11:05,001
I am at a loss to explain, captain.

207
00:11:05,013 --> 00:11:07,949
Well, why don't we pay our guest
a little visit and get some answers.

208
00:11:09,001 --> 00:11:10,678
Scotty.
You've got the conn.

209
00:11:24,078 --> 00:11:26,587
Captain, Dr. Elise McKennah. I
was recently assigned here...

210
00:11:26,610 --> 00:11:28,853
Now's not a good time, doctor.

211
00:11:29,160 --> 00:11:30,062
How is he, Bones?

212
00:11:30,073 --> 00:11:33,299
It's damn peculiar, Jim. His vitals
are stable... for now.

213
00:11:33,419 --> 00:11:36,235
Can't tell how long though.
Especially in the condition he's in.

214
00:11:36,355 --> 00:11:37,091
Condition?

215
00:11:37,211 --> 00:11:38,328
McCOY:
He's dying.

216
00:11:38,448 --> 00:11:40,114
Like any mortal human.

217
00:11:40,144 --> 00:11:40,967
SPOCK:
Fascinating.

218
00:11:41,087 --> 00:11:43,339
Oh and that organ I detected
on Pollux 4?

219
00:11:43,571 --> 00:11:48,612
It's still there, but it's barely detectable.
I had to run two bioscans just to find it.

220
00:11:49,635 --> 00:11:50,930
Captain Kirk.

221
00:11:55,249 --> 00:11:56,682
KIRK:
So you remember me.

222
00:11:57,653 --> 00:11:58,573
APOLLO:
Yes.

223
00:12:00,145 --> 00:12:05,417
You have extended an undeserved
kindness considering our history.

224
00:12:06,064 --> 00:12:07,599
For that I am grateful.

225
00:12:07,905 --> 00:12:09,421
Don't mention it.

226
00:12:10,235 --> 00:12:12,187
What's the meaning of
the attack on my ship?

227
00:12:13,065 --> 00:12:14,638
The attack was not my doing.

228
00:12:15,718 --> 00:12:18,270
When we sensed your
weapon approaching.

229
00:12:18,788 --> 00:12:21,720
Athena sacrificed her last
portion of energy...

230
00:12:22,383 --> 00:12:25,007
...to allow us both to escape
the Realm.

231
00:12:25,735 --> 00:12:26,545
Realm?

232
00:12:27,100 --> 00:12:31,377
It was concieved by our race eons ago
to be a place of eternal respite.

233
00:12:32,269 --> 00:12:37,645
The Realm was to provide the energy
for us to live on through eternity.

234
00:12:38,049 --> 00:12:40,581
Clearly it did not operate
as intended.

235
00:12:40,820 --> 00:12:42,010
No...

236
00:12:42,457 --> 00:12:44,503
Hera,
Poseidon,

237
00:12:45,671 --> 00:12:47,486
and my beloved Artemis...

238
00:12:48,087 --> 00:12:50,098
...were the first to arrive.

239
00:12:50,640 --> 00:12:53,750
The Realm drained them
of their life energy until...

240
00:12:54,686 --> 00:12:56,709
...they were too weak to escape.

241
00:12:57,491 --> 00:13:02,693
A malfunction caused it to siphon
power from any nearby energy source.

242
00:13:02,813 --> 00:13:05,800
Whether organic or artificial.

243
00:13:05,920 --> 00:13:07,264
The power stations...

244
00:13:07,384 --> 00:13:09,088
And your rapid aging.

245
00:13:09,879 --> 00:13:14,197
Even a god... without worship...

246
00:13:14,564 --> 00:13:16,625
...quickly withers.

247
00:13:17,161 --> 00:13:22,975
Because I waited for you, my
children, I was the last to arrive.

248
00:13:23,822 --> 00:13:27,096
I was ill-prepared for
what I discovered.

249
00:13:27,216 --> 00:13:28,733
They were dying.

250
00:13:28,853 --> 00:13:30,003
APOLLO:
Yes.

251
00:13:30,123 --> 00:13:35,028
What was meant to be our
sanctuary... became our prison.

252
00:13:35,148 --> 00:13:39,417
One by one, my brothers and sisters
slipped into oblivion...

253
00:13:39,537 --> 00:13:42,211
And I was powerless to save them.

254
00:13:44,243 --> 00:13:46,417
I sympathize, Apollo.

255
00:13:47,179 --> 00:13:48,675
But nothing has changed.

256
00:13:49,070 --> 00:13:51,441
We can't give you the
worship you want.

257
00:13:51,561 --> 00:13:53,416
Much has changed.

258
00:13:53,536 --> 00:13:55,816
I no longer seek it.

259
00:13:56,719 --> 00:13:58,187
Time...

260
00:13:58,307 --> 00:14:03,634
...who never dared to speak my
name... now pursues me.

261
00:14:05,819 --> 00:14:09,348
Does that surprise you?
Even please you?

262
00:14:09,799 --> 00:14:12,396
To know that I fear
passing into oblivion?

263
00:14:12,516 --> 00:14:14,598
No. It doesn't please us...

264
00:14:14,718 --> 00:14:17,053
...but your presence on this
ship is a great concern.

265
00:14:17,173 --> 00:14:19,170
Then hear my plea.

266
00:14:21,061 --> 00:14:25,097
I ask you to take me to a
world where mortals exist.

267
00:14:25,533 --> 00:14:30,218
So I feel the warmth of the sun...
the evening breeze on my face...

268
00:14:30,338 --> 00:14:32,843
No. So you can play god to
another unsuspecting race?

269
00:14:32,963 --> 00:14:35,242
I swear upon all Olympus...

270
00:14:35,637 --> 00:14:37,839
...upon the holy temple itself.

271
00:14:38,444 --> 00:14:43,412
I only desire to spend my remaining
days among them...

272
00:14:44,004 --> 00:14:45,472
...as one of them.

273
00:14:45,592 --> 00:14:49,282
I'm afraid that's a big risk.
And not one I'm prepared to take.

274
00:14:52,331 --> 00:14:58,484
To be a god for centuries only to
weaken and die alone...

275
00:15:00,823 --> 00:15:02,912
After my actions towards you...

276
00:15:03,956 --> 00:15:08,021
...perhaps you believe that is
what I deserve.

277
00:15:10,702 --> 00:15:12,791
Jim.
Perhaps we should let him rest.

278
00:15:18,436 --> 00:15:20,384
KIRK:
Check on Sulu's progress.

279
00:15:20,504 --> 00:15:22,105
Then I want to see the officers
in the briefing room.

280
00:15:22,225 --> 00:15:22,924
SPOCK:
Yes sir.

281
00:15:23,044 --> 00:15:24,124
Bones...

282
00:15:24,730 --> 00:15:26,710
Make sure Apollo is
what he claims.

283
00:15:26,830 --> 00:15:27,909
What's that?

284
00:15:28,834 --> 00:15:30,005
Powerless.

285
00:15:35,264 --> 00:15:36,006
McKENNA:
Captain...

286
00:15:36,195 --> 00:15:37,694
Yes... Doctor McKennah...
welcome aboard.

287
00:15:37,731 --> 00:15:39,668
Thank you, sir. I'm very anxious to
talk about this new position...

288
00:15:39,696 --> 00:15:40,319
Of course you are.

289
00:15:40,357 --> 00:15:42,936
For the time being, I need you to help
Dr. McCoy with our guest.

290
00:15:43,676 --> 00:15:44,951
Yes, sir.

291
00:16:06,791 --> 00:16:11,224
SULU:
A few more minutes of this and we can go inside
and use the shield array to scrape off the rest.

292
00:16:11,770 --> 00:16:13,839
SIMONE:
You love this stuff, don't you.

293
00:16:13,959 --> 00:16:16,752
No matter how hard we train, I
can't get used to assignments like this.

294
00:16:16,872 --> 00:16:18,935
Give me artificial gravity any day.

295
00:16:19,730 --> 00:16:21,663
SULU:
Artificial gravity...

296
00:16:30,210 --> 00:16:31,733
I'm Doctor McKennah.

297
00:16:31,853 --> 00:16:33,689
Are you feeling up for
some company?

298
00:16:34,369 --> 00:16:37,325
The company of a beautiful woman is
always welcome.

299
00:16:40,053 --> 00:16:43,167
You're just as charming as she
said you were.

300
00:16:43,287 --> 00:16:44,213
She?

301
00:16:45,213 --> 00:16:46,532
Lt. Palamas.

302
00:16:48,669 --> 00:16:49,919
Carolyn.

303
00:16:50,783 --> 00:16:52,283
Carolyn!

304
00:16:54,113 --> 00:16:58,115
The only mortal woman to
ever spurn my love.

305
00:16:58,592 --> 00:17:01,843
She did so out of dedication
for her crewmates.

306
00:17:02,479 --> 00:17:06,298
She also said it was the most
difficult thing she'd ever done.

307
00:17:06,418 --> 00:17:09,117
Then why does she not
declare this to me herself?

308
00:17:10,641 --> 00:17:12,141
She died.

309
00:17:13,800 --> 00:17:15,733
Carolyn, gone as well?

310
00:17:16,147 --> 00:17:19,625
After her experience with you she
decided to leave the ship.

311
00:17:20,170 --> 00:17:23,671
She died while helping some colonists
who were infected by a plague.

312
00:17:24,422 --> 00:17:26,945
Such sacrifice by such beauty.

313
00:17:27,786 --> 00:17:30,946
She's even more than I immediately
knew her to be.

314
00:17:31,628 --> 00:17:33,628
A rare treasure, McKennah.

315
00:17:37,632 --> 00:17:38,678
Elise.

316
00:17:51,682 --> 00:17:54,341
SCOTT (V.O.):
Mr. Sulu. I'm reading increased
hull temperature in your area.

317
00:17:54,461 --> 00:17:57,115
Reduce phaser strength
by 20 percent to compensate.

318
00:17:57,903 --> 00:17:59,313
SULU:
That's gonna slow us down.

319
00:17:59,433 --> 00:18:01,154
SCOTT (V.O.):
Aye laddie. It will at that.

320
00:18:01,274 --> 00:18:03,723
But better to err on the side of
caution. Wouldn't you agree?

321
00:18:08,201 --> 00:18:10,429
SULU:
Simone. You heard him. Compensate.

322
00:18:10,549 --> 00:18:12,180
SIMONE:
I'm trying it's not working.

323
00:18:13,589 --> 00:18:15,794
Enterprise. We may have
a problem out here.

324
00:18:15,914 --> 00:18:17,428
Tell me...

325
00:18:17,974 --> 00:18:21,429
Why didn't you and the other gods
find another planet with humans...

326
00:18:21,549 --> 00:18:23,316
...where you could be worshipped?

327
00:18:24,043 --> 00:18:26,794
So deep was our love for humanity...

328
00:18:26,914 --> 00:18:29,886
...and our heartbreak when
they rejected us...

329
00:18:30,386 --> 00:18:33,296
We couldn't bear the risk of
such heartbreak again.

330
00:18:33,682 --> 00:18:36,047
Our only choice
was the Realm.

331
00:18:37,262 --> 00:18:42,082
Of all of us, none loved
you humans more than I.

332
00:18:44,264 --> 00:18:47,492
You must understand the
captain's concerns.

333
00:18:48,811 --> 00:18:51,766
If only I could open up my
heart and show him...

334
00:18:52,130 --> 00:18:53,880
Show all of you...

335
00:18:54,812 --> 00:18:57,699
All I desire is life and peace.

336
00:19:10,306 --> 00:19:11,898
SULU:
Simone!

337
00:19:17,558 --> 00:19:18,604
Enterprise!

338
00:19:18,724 --> 00:19:19,745
Emergency!

339
00:19:23,541 --> 00:19:25,655
Enterprise!
Emergency beam-out!

340
00:19:39,738 --> 00:19:40,488
KIRK:
Sulu!

341
00:19:40,608 --> 00:19:41,557
McCOY:
Get that helmet off.

342
00:19:43,762 --> 00:19:45,194
SCOTT:
Are you all right, lad?

343
00:19:49,741 --> 00:19:50,854
McCOY:
Slow, easy breaths, Sulu.

344
00:19:51,627 --> 00:19:52,628
Better now.

345
00:19:52,748 --> 00:19:53,446
Scotty?

346
00:19:53,566 --> 00:19:56,129
Drained of power, captain.
This was no bloody malfunction.

347
00:19:56,249 --> 00:19:58,175
That monster lying in sickbay.
I lay odds on his doing.

348
00:19:58,295 --> 00:19:59,493
KIRK:
Apollo is in no condition...

349
00:19:59,613 --> 00:20:01,927
Remember what he did to Carolyn.
And now he's killed Simone!

350
00:20:02,314 --> 00:20:04,269
SULU:
Everything was going
as planned, captain.

351
00:20:04,769 --> 00:20:06,588
Simone was trying
to compensate.

352
00:20:06,929 --> 00:20:07,975
His phaser...

353
00:20:08,406 --> 00:20:09,520
I tried to help him.

354
00:20:09,884 --> 00:20:11,180
But there was nothing
I could do!

355
00:20:11,300 --> 00:20:12,839
It's all right, Sulu. It's all right.

356
00:20:13,521 --> 00:20:14,317
Bones?

357
00:20:14,437 --> 00:20:17,500
He's okay. A bit shaken, but no
permanent damage.

358
00:20:17,620 --> 00:20:20,842
I'm fine, captain.
But Simone... his body...

359
00:20:20,962 --> 00:20:22,252
KIRK:
We'll bring him home.

360
00:20:22,638 --> 00:20:24,412
You get some rest.

361
00:20:24,843 --> 00:20:27,026
Return to duty when you're
feeling up to it. All right?

362
00:20:28,049 --> 00:20:30,095
Bones, Scotty. You're with me.

363
00:20:42,771 --> 00:20:44,749
SCOTT:
It's him! He's to blame
for all of this!

364
00:20:44,869 --> 00:20:47,954
KIRK:
There's no reason to believe that this was
anything more than a tragic accident.

365
00:20:48,074 --> 00:20:51,251
SCOTT:
I tell ya he's behind it somehow!
He wants us all on our knees prayin' to him!

366
00:20:51,371 --> 00:20:54,138
It would be illogical for
Apollo to kill us.

367
00:20:54,258 --> 00:20:57,161
Especially considering how
desperate he seems for our help.

368
00:20:57,525 --> 00:20:58,662
I agree with Spock.

369
00:21:01,488 --> 00:21:04,284
A decision needs to be made about
Apollo and what do to with him.

370
00:21:04,716 --> 00:21:08,126
As my senior officers I'm bringing you
in on that decision with me.

371
00:21:08,921 --> 00:21:11,467
McCOY:
Right now Apollo's in no
condition to do anything.

372
00:21:11,587 --> 00:21:14,014
Maybe what he claims is true.

373
00:21:14,134 --> 00:21:16,537
Dr. McCoy.
You can't be serious.

374
00:21:16,657 --> 00:21:19,742
Simone would be alive right now if
that creature hadn't come aboard.

375
00:21:19,862 --> 00:21:21,743
Dr. McCoy's point is valid.

376
00:21:21,863 --> 00:21:26,364
Apollo's appearance here could be --
as he said -- a matter of survival.

377
00:21:26,484 --> 00:21:28,410
He is now the last of his race.

378
00:21:28,728 --> 00:21:30,365
I sympathize, gentlemen.

379
00:21:31,251 --> 00:21:34,002
But not to the point where the
safety of the crew is in jeopardy.

380
00:21:35,343 --> 00:21:37,344
We have no further facts
at this time, captain.

381
00:21:37,464 --> 00:21:40,777
And he hasn't demonstrated any
aggressiveness toward anyone, Jim.

382
00:21:40,897 --> 00:21:43,551
We're the ones keeping him
locked up in sickbay.

383
00:21:44,028 --> 00:21:48,029
Captain, if our objective is to
determine Apollo's true intentions...

384
00:21:48,149 --> 00:21:52,690
...perhaps it is time we -- what is the
saying -- "give him enough rope"?

385
00:21:53,326 --> 00:21:54,667
Something like that.

386
00:21:54,787 --> 00:21:55,686
I don't believe it!

387
00:21:55,806 --> 00:21:57,278
That'll be enough, Mr. Scott.

388
00:22:01,796 --> 00:22:03,683
Aye, sir.

389
00:22:04,615 --> 00:22:06,957
If you'll excuse me.

390
00:22:07,775 --> 00:22:11,071
I need to continue repairing the
damage caused by our guest.

391
00:22:14,640 --> 00:22:16,709
McCOY:
Scotty's not as forgiving
as some, Jim.

392
00:22:17,482 --> 00:22:20,801
Seeing Apollo again has really
reopened some old deep wounds.

393
00:22:20,921 --> 00:22:22,729
He doesn't trust him.

394
00:22:23,298 --> 00:22:25,071
Can't say I really
blame him.

395
00:22:27,708 --> 00:22:30,572
Have the security detail
accompany Apollo.

396
00:22:30,913 --> 00:22:33,005
And allow him to walk
about the ship a bit.

397
00:22:33,323 --> 00:22:34,232
And Spock?

398
00:22:34,352 --> 00:22:35,414
Yes captain?

399
00:22:35,801 --> 00:22:37,071
Make it a short leash.

400
00:22:48,796 --> 00:22:51,962
APOLLO:
Many things have changed on
your Earth over the centuries.

401
00:22:53,221 --> 00:22:54,338
Some things have not.

402
00:22:54,458 --> 00:22:56,154
Well one thing has changed...

403
00:22:56,274 --> 00:22:58,587
I've one less crewman than
I did an hour ago.

404
00:22:58,707 --> 00:23:01,822
Perhaps you can tell me how your Realm
managed to kill one of my men.

405
00:23:01,942 --> 00:23:03,329
I have little knowledge of this matter...

406
00:23:03,449 --> 00:23:05,366
That's not good enough.

407
00:23:06,122 --> 00:23:09,824
Look. If you want our help, you're going to
have to start providing some answers.

408
00:23:09,944 --> 00:23:12,459
The Realm thrives on energy...

409
00:23:13,150 --> 00:23:16,223
Survival became its sole purpose.

410
00:23:16,343 --> 00:23:18,715
Athena and I were its prisoners.

411
00:23:18,835 --> 00:23:21,224
Had your ship not been so close...

412
00:23:21,344 --> 00:23:23,770
...we would have been
unable to escape.

413
00:23:25,637 --> 00:23:28,547
Perhaps you have reconsidered
my request.

414
00:23:29,602 --> 00:23:34,476
I have a responsibility to my Federation's
non-interference directive.

415
00:23:34,596 --> 00:23:39,095
To allow someone with your history to descend
upon some planet and set up church...

416
00:23:39,215 --> 00:23:43,520
Your history tapes show how humans
have evolved as a species.

417
00:23:43,640 --> 00:23:47,066
Yet your Federation's promise of
compassion and benevolence...

418
00:23:47,186 --> 00:23:49,031
...seem little more than words!

419
00:23:51,213 --> 00:23:53,684
Is that all you learned
from our history?

420
00:23:54,767 --> 00:23:57,952
I assure you our decision will be a
just one. In the meantime...

421
00:23:58,407 --> 00:24:00,444
If you're feeling up to it... You may...

422
00:24:00,753 --> 00:24:02,172
...walk about the ship.

423
00:24:02,292 --> 00:24:05,827
Get to know present-day humanity
from some of its best representatives...

424
00:24:05,947 --> 00:24:09,228
My crew.
Accompanied by security, of course.

425
00:24:09,348 --> 00:24:10,374
Of course.

426
00:24:11,430 --> 00:24:13,413
I'm pleased to see...

427
00:24:14,213 --> 00:24:16,195
...I was remembered.

428
00:24:17,414 --> 00:24:20,814
Yes... you were remembered...
but not as a deity.

429
00:24:46,209 --> 00:24:51,517
UHURA:
♪ Travel, my love,
to the end of the stars... ♪

430
00:24:51,637 --> 00:24:57,022
♪ ...and know you are with me,
adored from afar. ♪

431
00:24:57,883 --> 00:25:02,679
♪ One starry night,
on the moon of SaDun... ♪

432
00:25:03,105 --> 00:25:09,634
♪ ...we'll meet and we'll fly off,
forever as one. ♪

433
00:25:20,677 --> 00:25:24,090
I knew this was important to you,
so I wanted to keep it safe.

434
00:25:24,210 --> 00:25:28,694
You will honor me,
if you continue to do so.

435
00:25:29,989 --> 00:25:32,518
Y'know, you have the voice of an angel.

436
00:25:33,525 --> 00:25:34,564
Thank you.

437
00:25:34,684 --> 00:25:36,383
As that of another...

438
00:25:36,503 --> 00:25:38,514
My sister, Artemis.

439
00:25:39,992 --> 00:25:41,981
Thank you!

440
00:25:42,493 --> 00:25:46,187
Wait... You're the Greek god of music.

441
00:25:46,670 --> 00:25:48,148
I am.

442
00:25:50,535 --> 00:25:51,956
I was.

443
00:25:52,076 --> 00:25:53,973
I'm sorry I didn't mean to...

444
00:25:54,093 --> 00:25:57,099
No, no.
It's all right, my child.

445
00:25:57,549 --> 00:26:02,096
Even through the centuries,
music stills echoes in my mind...

446
00:26:02,216 --> 00:26:06,415
The battles, heroes,
bravery... love.

447
00:26:07,126 --> 00:26:09,371
To love a mortal was...

448
00:26:09,491 --> 00:26:12,838
...to watch the sun rise
on a perfect day.

449
00:26:13,463 --> 00:26:16,702
Only to mourn too
quickly at setting...

450
00:26:16,822 --> 00:26:19,260
...into eternal night.

451
00:26:22,835 --> 00:26:28,003
♪ Walk, you and I,
through the gardens of old... ♪

452
00:26:28,992 --> 00:26:35,409
♪ Adorn thou, my love,
in silver and gold. ♪

453
00:26:35,529 --> 00:26:42,070
♪ Drink of sweet wine,
as brave tales are told. ♪

454
00:26:42,775 --> 00:26:48,299
♪ Pray to the gods that we may... ♪

455
00:26:48,419 --> 00:26:56,280
♪ Love a thousand years in a day. ♪

456
00:27:01,288 --> 00:27:04,637
Thank you. Thank you.
You are very kind.

457
00:27:05,092 --> 00:27:08,766
It pleases me that you still
enjoy my music, my children.

458
00:27:09,057 --> 00:27:10,748
That was just lovely.

459
00:27:11,075 --> 00:27:12,039
Y'know...

460
00:27:12,366 --> 00:27:15,967
Marsyas once challenged me
to a contest of music.

461
00:27:16,087 --> 00:27:17,362
CREWMAN:
Who is Marsyas?

462
00:27:17,482 --> 00:27:23,357
Marsyas was a satyr who believed he could
play his flute as proficiently as I my lyre.

463
00:27:23,902 --> 00:27:27,576
After we both played,
the Muses declared...

464
00:27:27,696 --> 00:27:29,940
...the contest a tie.

465
00:27:30,286 --> 00:27:32,050
Until I decreed...

466
00:27:32,170 --> 00:27:35,351
...that we both play and sing at
the same time!

467
00:27:36,970 --> 00:27:39,534
An impossible feat
for Marsyas!

468
00:27:43,190 --> 00:27:44,851
[INTERCOM WHISTLE]

469
00:27:44,971 --> 00:27:46,996
SCOTT (V.O.):
Engineering to Captain Kirk.

470
00:27:49,551 --> 00:27:50,287
Kirk here.

471
00:27:50,340 --> 00:27:51,373
Scott here, sir.

472
00:27:51,493 --> 00:27:53,817
My panels are lighting up
with malfunctions everywhere.

473
00:27:53,842 --> 00:27:56,728
Some of that corrosive junk definitely
got into some of our systems.

474
00:27:56,755 --> 00:27:57,813
How bad is it?

475
00:27:57,838 --> 00:27:59,219
I don't know yet.

476
00:27:59,339 --> 00:28:02,107
I'm working on a way to rig a ship-wide
electromagnetic pulse...

477
00:28:02,227 --> 00:28:04,097
...to wipe it out all in one shot.

478
00:28:05,304 --> 00:28:06,386
Well...

479
00:28:06,770 --> 00:28:09,273
Do what you can, Scotty.
Keep me informed. Kirk out.

480
00:28:09,610 --> 00:28:10,774
[DOOR BUZZER]

481
00:28:10,894 --> 00:28:11,589
Come.

482
00:28:12,462 --> 00:28:14,126
Captain. I thought now...

483
00:28:17,224 --> 00:28:18,702
Yes, lieutenant?

484
00:28:20,332 --> 00:28:23,149
I thought now would be a good
time for that little chat.

485
00:28:24,382 --> 00:28:26,303
Well, you're certainly persistent.

486
00:28:27,632 --> 00:28:29,086
I get that a lot.

487
00:28:30,937 --> 00:28:32,415
Give me one minute.

488
00:28:38,609 --> 00:28:41,344
I suppose you feel like
you need to prove yourself...

489
00:28:41,464 --> 00:28:44,720
...being Starfleet's first onboard
ship's counselor.

490
00:28:44,840 --> 00:28:49,709
Not, I assume, unlike you feeling you had to
prove yourself when you became captain at 33.

491
00:28:50,018 --> 00:28:53,237
The youngest starship captain
in Starfleet history.

492
00:28:53,357 --> 00:28:54,783
32.

493
00:28:56,256 --> 00:28:58,166
But close enough.

494
00:28:59,366 --> 00:29:00,621
My mistake.

495
00:29:01,883 --> 00:29:06,303
Captain, part of my job is to assess the
psychological condition of all onboard.

496
00:29:06,423 --> 00:29:10,776
Even alien life forms like Apollo.
And make recommendations.

497
00:29:11,104 --> 00:29:13,759
And...
I'll bet you have one.

498
00:29:14,086 --> 00:29:15,759
Yes. I do.

499
00:29:18,128 --> 00:29:19,983
I believe him.

500
00:29:20,438 --> 00:29:23,639
I believe that he is sincere
about everything that he says.

501
00:29:24,042 --> 00:29:26,803
Whatever Apollo might have
been once upon a time...

502
00:29:27,076 --> 00:29:30,349
...his experiences since
then have changed him.

503
00:29:30,469 --> 00:29:32,295
But changed him how?

504
00:29:32,983 --> 00:29:37,615
That's what I need to know before I could
even consider granting his request.

505
00:29:37,735 --> 00:29:42,276
If I'm wrong, there's no telling
what he's liable to do.

506
00:29:42,396 --> 00:29:45,544
But can't that be said
about any one of us?

507
00:29:45,664 --> 00:29:49,096
In each of us there's the
potential to do great things...

508
00:29:49,216 --> 00:29:52,733
And to do dangerous and
unpredictable things.

509
00:29:52,853 --> 00:29:54,893
How is Apollo any different?

510
00:29:55,013 --> 00:29:59,070
Last time I checked, neither you nor I
where ever worshipped as gods.

511
00:29:59,433 --> 00:30:01,593
I'd call that a big difference.

512
00:30:02,048 --> 00:30:06,452
He watched his friends die
in that Realm.

513
00:30:07,277 --> 00:30:09,777
And now he's here.

514
00:30:09,897 --> 00:30:15,262
Alone.
And he's facing death himself.

515
00:30:16,029 --> 00:30:18,405
I truly believe that if
we let the...

516
00:30:18,435 --> 00:30:20,604
[INTERCOM WHISTLES]

517
00:30:22,139 --> 00:30:22,963
Kirk here.

518
00:30:23,083 --> 00:30:24,213
SPOCK (V.O.):
Spock here, Captain.

519
00:30:24,333 --> 00:30:27,551
I suggest you come to rec room 6
as soon as possible.

520
00:30:27,671 --> 00:30:28,716
Why? What is it?

521
00:30:28,836 --> 00:30:31,558
I think you should see for yourself.

522
00:30:32,788 --> 00:30:33,746
On my way.

523
00:30:36,053 --> 00:30:41,603
When Troilus, the young Trojan prince,
rejected Achilles' lustful advances...

524
00:30:41,723 --> 00:30:45,377
He sealed the fate of
himself and his city.

525
00:30:45,497 --> 00:30:48,929
Achilles arrived at Troy
with 50 ships.

526
00:30:49,049 --> 00:30:52,792
His arrogance only
matched by his rage.

527
00:30:52,912 --> 00:30:56,700
On that fateful day,
when Paris fired his arrow...

528
00:30:56,820 --> 00:31:00,563
It was I who guided it
into Achilles' heel.

529
00:31:00,683 --> 00:31:04,803
Payment for the sacrilege of
murdering Troilus, my son!

530
00:31:04,923 --> 00:31:06,153
On the very altar of...

531
00:31:06,180 --> 00:31:07,072
Apollo!

532
00:31:12,147 --> 00:31:14,478
I'd like a word, please.

533
00:31:15,200 --> 00:31:16,522
Outside.

534
00:31:38,566 --> 00:31:41,563
What was that back there?
Your idea of Olympian honor?

535
00:31:41,683 --> 00:31:44,615
You assured us you
had no desire for worship.

536
00:31:44,735 --> 00:31:47,834
This is a pretty poor way of proving
yourself worthy of our help.

537
00:31:47,954 --> 00:31:49,372
It is you who are not worthy.

538
00:31:49,492 --> 00:31:51,037
If you expect us to...

539
00:32:23,518 --> 00:32:27,069
Is this a preview of what you'll do
to some unsuspecting planet?

540
00:32:27,723 --> 00:32:31,331
Well you'll never get there! And you'll
die alone! Is that what you want?

541
00:32:31,451 --> 00:32:34,838
I want what is mine! 
Humanity's worship and love!

542
00:32:34,958 --> 00:32:36,404
Humanity never loved you!

543
00:32:37,400 --> 00:32:38,761
They feared you!

544
00:32:38,881 --> 00:32:40,552
No god should survive on fear!

545
00:32:40,672 --> 00:32:41,402
Enough!

546
00:32:41,837 --> 00:32:44,321
Receive the wage of your blasphemy!

547
00:33:12,815 --> 00:33:14,140
You're going to the brig.

548
00:33:14,178 --> 00:33:15,253
Take him to sickbay.

549
00:33:15,819 --> 00:33:18,734
Tell McCoy to put him in restraints 
and sedate him if he has to.

550
00:33:19,155 --> 00:33:20,108
Aye sir.

551
00:33:30,993 --> 00:33:32,188
Help Dr. McKennah.

552
00:33:33,186 --> 00:33:34,973
Come with me, Elise.

553
00:33:35,736 --> 00:33:38,221
All right, everyone.
Back to your stations.

554
00:33:43,453 --> 00:33:45,106
I guess Scotty was right.

555
00:33:45,226 --> 00:33:45,988
How so?

556
00:33:46,500 --> 00:33:48,584
You were here, Spock,
You saw what just happened.

557
00:33:48,704 --> 00:33:49,626
Yes sir.

558
00:33:49,746 --> 00:33:53,350
While Apollo clearly had influence on 
those in the recreation room, I...

559
00:33:53,669 --> 00:33:55,924
...do not believe he intended to
behave as he did.

560
00:33:56,733 --> 00:33:58,499
So you think he 
can't control it?

561
00:33:58,619 --> 00:34:02,684
He's been this divine parasite for so long
that he just can't help himself, is that it?

562
00:34:02,804 --> 00:34:06,071
It has been my experience that 
humans -- if not most beings --

563
00:34:06,114 --> 00:34:10,190
...are unable to change their behaviors 
that are deeply embedded by choice.

564
00:34:10,466 --> 00:34:13,637
It is often forced by tragedy 
or outside intervention.

565
00:34:13,757 --> 00:34:17,072
Well Apollo's therapy will not be at the 
expense of the crew of the Enterprise.

566
00:34:17,098 --> 00:34:18,485
I can promise you that.

567
00:34:18,989 --> 00:34:21,948
Apparently you can't teach
an old god new tricks.

568
00:34:31,557 --> 00:34:33,784
Easy, Jim.
He's restrained.

569
00:34:33,904 --> 00:34:36,577
He offered his wrists for the straps.

570
00:34:42,993 --> 00:34:44,955
What words can I offer, captain?

571
00:34:45,075 --> 00:34:46,827
How about an explanation?

572
00:34:46,947 --> 00:34:48,988
Even a god can lose control.

573
00:34:49,108 --> 00:34:51,282
But you said you weren't a god anmyore.

574
00:34:51,402 --> 00:34:52,711
I'm not.

575
00:34:54,174 --> 00:34:56,235
I'm a fool.

576
00:34:57,100 --> 00:35:00,424
I believed I could control the
deepest ancient drives...

577
00:35:00,956 --> 00:35:03,150
...by sheer force of my will.

578
00:35:03,270 --> 00:35:05,345
I see now I cannot.

579
00:35:06,705 --> 00:35:08,667
You will not help me then, will you?

580
00:35:08,787 --> 00:35:12,490
That little show of yours demonstrated
exactly why I can't.

581
00:35:12,610 --> 00:35:13,920
Nor should you.

582
00:35:15,748 --> 00:35:17,244
I'm sorry.

583
00:35:18,341 --> 00:35:20,170
So am I.

584
00:35:21,666 --> 00:35:24,791
But I don't see any alternative.

585
00:35:25,255 --> 00:35:26,652
Wait!

586
00:35:29,444 --> 00:35:32,369
The organ in my body.

587
00:35:32,489 --> 00:35:35,228
That's what allows you to channel the
power from that temple of yours.

588
00:35:35,348 --> 00:35:40,082
Yes. Yes.
But it is serves another purpose.

589
00:35:42,812 --> 00:35:43,806
I'm listening.

590
00:35:43,926 --> 00:35:46,964
It's what converts mortal
worship into energy.

591
00:35:47,535 --> 00:35:50,660
An energy that defies your understanding.

592
00:35:51,325 --> 00:35:54,682
I cannot control it as I first believed.

593
00:35:56,012 --> 00:35:58,240
Neither will I let it control me.

594
00:35:58,360 --> 00:36:02,196
"If thine eye offends thee, 
then pluck it out..."

595
00:36:02,595 --> 00:36:05,022
It's a quote from one of 
our greatest Earth books.

596
00:36:05,142 --> 00:36:08,516
You want me to remove the organ.

597
00:36:08,636 --> 00:36:10,345
Yes.

598
00:36:10,810 --> 00:36:12,339
Can you do it, Bones?

599
00:36:12,459 --> 00:36:14,334
Well I dunno, Jim. It's...

600
00:36:14,454 --> 00:36:17,154
Removing an organ is straightforward
enough stuff, but considering...

601
00:36:17,217 --> 00:36:18,528
I accept any risk.

602
00:36:19,975 --> 00:36:22,953
My powers must be extinguished permanently.

603
00:36:23,073 --> 00:36:25,320
If I am to survive.

604
00:36:25,826 --> 00:36:27,368
And no god...

605
00:36:28,352 --> 00:36:29,549
...no one...

606
00:36:30,879 --> 00:36:32,475
...should survive on fear.

607
00:36:34,017 --> 00:36:35,214
It's your call.

608
00:36:35,719 --> 00:36:37,209
Please, doctor...

609
00:36:38,805 --> 00:36:40,640
I have faith in you.

610
00:36:48,885 --> 00:36:50,507
KIRK (V.O.):
Captain's log, supplemental.

611
00:36:50,627 --> 00:36:53,518
While ship's surgeon McCoy performs
elective surgery on Apollo...

612
00:36:53,638 --> 00:36:56,838
...to remove what the medical staff has
deemed the "God Organ"...

613
00:36:56,958 --> 00:36:59,901
...Mr. Scott has come up with a
solution to our other problem.

614
00:37:00,021 --> 00:37:02,486
He and his engineering team
begin within the hour.

615
00:37:02,606 --> 00:37:03,677
[INTERCOM WHISTLE]

616
00:37:03,797 --> 00:37:05,841
McCOY:
Sickbay to Captain Kirk.

617
00:37:10,358 --> 00:37:11,486
Kirk here, Bones.

618
00:37:11,606 --> 00:37:12,996
Well... it's done, Jim.

619
00:37:13,815 --> 00:37:16,943
He's very weak and I couldn't give you 
a guess about a recovery.

620
00:37:17,432 --> 00:37:18,453
But it's out.

621
00:37:18,751 --> 00:37:19,609
Understood.

622
00:37:19,658 --> 00:37:21,921
Join us in the briefing room
as soon as you're able.

623
00:37:39,132 --> 00:37:40,749
Well, gentlemen.

624
00:37:40,869 --> 00:37:43,617
Thanks to some skillful work
by our good doctor...

625
00:37:43,737 --> 00:37:48,340
...it is probable that Apollo no longer has 
the urge to thrive on the worship of others.

626
00:37:48,744 --> 00:37:51,659
I would therefore like to revisit 
the question of resettling him.

627
00:37:51,779 --> 00:37:54,534
As with any surgery, we'll have 
to wait and see for a while...

628
00:37:54,556 --> 00:37:56,455
...but I'm fairly sure he's harmless now.

629
00:37:56,575 --> 00:38:00,627
I believe Apollo's sincerity and intention
were demonstrated by his willingness...

630
00:38:00,658 --> 00:38:04,099
...to have an organ that he has relied
for centuries removed.

631
00:38:04,423 --> 00:38:06,295
SCOTT:
Forgive me for saying so, gentlemen.

632
00:38:06,415 --> 00:38:08,690
But it seems like a lot 
of wishful thinking.

633
00:38:08,810 --> 00:38:13,424
We've nothing but Apollo's word that removing
that thing will make a wee bit of difference.

634
00:38:13,544 --> 00:38:15,525
And what would you suggest, Scotty?

635
00:38:17,652 --> 00:38:19,089
I don't know.

636
00:38:19,913 --> 00:38:24,304
I just feel that turning him loose
somewhere is certain disaster.

637
00:38:24,424 --> 00:38:27,443
So we still have no consensus.

638
00:38:29,384 --> 00:38:34,012
Bones. Keep observing Apollo.
Let me know if there're any changes.

639
00:38:35,315 --> 00:38:37,522
What's the status of your operation?

640
00:38:37,642 --> 00:38:40,478
We're ready to trigger the surge and
sweep the ship's systems of that gunk.

641
00:38:40,598 --> 00:38:43,962
I will monitor Mr. Scott's
progress from the bridge.

642
00:38:44,082 --> 00:38:45,105
How long?

643
00:38:45,225 --> 00:38:46,914
I can have it for
you in 10 minutes.

644
00:38:48,084 --> 00:38:51,406
10 minutes it is.
Dismissed, gentlemen.

645
00:38:56,725 --> 00:38:59,385
Offering himself up for major surgery...

646
00:39:00,874 --> 00:39:02,709
Maybe he is sincere, Bones.

647
00:39:02,829 --> 00:39:05,662
Maybe.
Maybe not.

648
00:39:05,782 --> 00:39:10,648
One thing's for certain, Jim. If we can't come
to a consensus soon it won't matter.

649
00:39:11,073 --> 00:39:16,385
Rather than feel the warmth of the sun,
Apollo will probably die in sickbay.

650
00:39:26,343 --> 00:39:29,053
Scott to bridge.
We're ready to begin.

651
00:39:29,173 --> 00:39:31,172
KIRK:
At your discretion, Mr. Scott.

652
00:39:39,774 --> 00:39:40,909
It's working, captain. 

653
00:39:41,029 --> 00:39:43,990
The pulse has so far eliminated
30 percent of the foreign substance.

654
00:39:44,258 --> 00:39:46,216
Well done, Scotty.

655
00:39:47,274 --> 00:39:48,728
Good riddance.

656
00:39:48,848 --> 00:39:50,723
Get off my ship.

657
00:39:57,871 --> 00:40:01,071
Bridge, I'm reading a buildup of
electrostatic energy in your systems.

658
00:40:01,191 --> 00:40:04,770
I advise that you step away from your stations
as a precaution while I try to compensate.

659
00:40:12,375 --> 00:40:13,372
Uhura!

660
00:40:14,535 --> 00:40:16,075
Uhura?

661
00:40:18,361 --> 00:40:19,067
Sulu!

662
00:40:19,187 --> 00:40:21,893
Tell McCoy we're coming.
You've got the conn.

663
00:40:28,584 --> 00:40:30,163
Bones!

664
00:40:37,195 --> 00:40:38,883
Stay with me, lieutenant.

665
00:40:41,545 --> 00:40:44,532
Stay with me.
Cortical stimulator.

666
00:40:46,214 --> 00:40:47,253
Bones.

667
00:41:01,165 --> 00:41:02,441
Jim, I...

668
00:41:21,654 --> 00:41:22,611
No, dammit!

669
00:41:22,628 --> 00:41:23,200
Doctor...

670
00:41:23,237 --> 00:41:24,718
No!
Get me the bypass...

671
00:41:24,838 --> 00:41:25,578
Bones.

672
00:42:09,982 --> 00:42:12,709
Apollo, I do not know what 
you hope to accomplish.

673
00:42:12,829 --> 00:42:15,956
But your recovery will require what 
little energy you have remaining.

674
00:42:28,899 --> 00:42:30,003
Nyota!

675
00:42:36,366 --> 00:42:37,470
What happened?

676
00:42:37,590 --> 00:42:38,639
It's all right.

677
00:42:52,472 --> 00:42:55,521
Removing that organ was supposed
to render him powerless.

678
00:42:56,309 --> 00:42:57,404
How did he do that?

679
00:42:57,524 --> 00:42:59,807
I can't tell you that if I was
from Mount Olympus myself.

680
00:42:59,927 --> 00:43:03,378
But he has more energy now
than he did before the surgery.

681
00:43:03,498 --> 00:43:04,937
Something, Spock?

682
00:43:05,057 --> 00:43:06,308
Is it possible...

683
00:43:06,346 --> 00:43:11,245
...that Apollo's species are able to draw
power from something other than worship?

684
00:43:12,109 --> 00:43:16,888
Well, Apollo himself said that it was the 
organ that converted worship into energy.

685
00:43:17,008 --> 00:43:18,655
Life energy, he said.

686
00:43:18,775 --> 00:43:20,577
What are you getting at, Spock?

687
00:43:21,965 --> 00:43:23,901
Sacrifice.

688
00:43:25,511 --> 00:43:27,278
Why not?

689
00:43:27,398 --> 00:43:32,940
We've seen species throughout the galaxy
that draw energy from many different sources.

690
00:43:33,060 --> 00:43:36,316
Well if that's the case,
why all this insistence on worship?

691
00:43:36,436 --> 00:43:38,273
Perhaps the gods assumed...

692
00:43:38,327 --> 00:43:43,125
...that since worship from humans empowered them,
that was the only thing that did so.

693
00:43:43,449 --> 00:43:45,511
Let's find out.

694
00:43:46,290 --> 00:43:47,329
How's he doing?

695
00:43:47,449 --> 00:43:49,251
He is fine.

696
00:43:49,371 --> 00:43:51,225
What you did... 

697
00:43:51,345 --> 00:43:52,731
That was really something.

698
00:43:52,851 --> 00:43:55,900
I don't fully understand it myself.

699
00:43:56,020 --> 00:44:01,666
But when I saw Lt. Uhura lying there...
and the grief on all your faces...

700
00:44:01,786 --> 00:44:05,614
...I realized the universe was a
better place with her in it.

701
00:44:05,734 --> 00:44:09,302
So you thought saving her would kill you.

702
00:44:10,415 --> 00:44:12,040
Well, not only did you not die...

703
00:44:12,081 --> 00:44:18,252
...but we believe the energy you gained
actually came from your act of sacrifice.

704
00:44:19,142 --> 00:44:20,701
Do you mean to say that...?

705
00:44:20,821 --> 00:44:23,246
All those centuries demanding worship...

706
00:44:23,366 --> 00:44:26,778
Forcing adoration.
Striking those that resisted.

707
00:44:26,898 --> 00:44:31,765
You never considered there 
may be another way. A better way.

708
00:44:32,440 --> 00:44:36,129
No.
We did not.

709
00:44:36,249 --> 00:44:38,990
It is unlikely that sacrificial acts alone...

710
00:44:39,013 --> 00:44:41,271
...will provide you with the
energy you once enjoyed.

711
00:44:41,391 --> 00:44:43,713
So... no more thunderbolts.

712
00:44:43,833 --> 00:44:46,371
But if you continue in this manner...

713
00:44:46,491 --> 00:44:50,285
...it is conceivable that you could
prolong your life indefinitely.

714
00:44:52,465 --> 00:44:55,270
We never knew.

715
00:44:55,842 --> 00:44:59,634
I'd like to see the senior officers
in the briefing room.

716
00:45:00,480 --> 00:45:04,049
And... 
Keep an eye on him.

717
00:45:12,826 --> 00:45:14,946
And that, gentlemen,
is where we stand.

718
00:45:15,066 --> 00:45:20,099
I believe Apollo has demonstrated that he can
live peaceably among humanoids...

719
00:45:20,219 --> 00:45:21,429
...as one of them.

720
00:45:21,549 --> 00:45:25,792
Our present course will take us
past the Basilean Epsilon system.

721
00:45:25,912 --> 00:45:29,989
The second planet is a simple
humanoid population.

722
00:45:30,832 --> 00:45:34,282
Apollo sacrificed himself to save
one of our own...

723
00:45:34,822 --> 00:45:37,481
...and now he's placed 
his fate in our hands.

724
00:45:38,188 --> 00:45:41,388
All those in favor of finding
him a new home?

725
00:46:09,013 --> 00:46:11,506
This will be a 
new experience for you.

726
00:46:11,626 --> 00:46:13,459
And I am eager for it.

727
00:46:13,579 --> 00:46:17,864
Captain Kirk. 
You and your crew have my deepest thanks.

728
00:46:17,984 --> 00:46:21,230
None of this would have been
possible without your enlightenment.

729
00:46:21,350 --> 00:46:23,765
Well... that's what we mortals do.

730
00:46:23,885 --> 00:46:27,464
You'll find the inhabitants of this
planet to be a simple people...

731
00:46:27,584 --> 00:46:31,370
...at a cultural development stage similar
to the Earth's 14th century.

732
00:46:31,490 --> 00:46:35,895
Which means you'll have plenty of opportunities
to practice that new-found gift of yours.

733
00:46:36,015 --> 00:46:40,051
And I shall do so with all of my heart.
I swear it.

734
00:46:50,676 --> 00:46:52,172
Thank you.

735
00:46:55,953 --> 00:46:57,740
Thank you.

736
00:46:59,652 --> 00:47:01,647
God speed, Apollo.

737
00:47:05,761 --> 00:47:06,966
Energize.

738
00:47:21,164 --> 00:47:24,115
KIRK (V.O.):
Captain's log, stardate 6147.9.

739
00:47:24,235 --> 00:47:28,894
Mr. Scott reports that repairs have begun on the
3 affected power stations in this quadrant.

740
00:47:29,014 --> 00:47:31,927
They are scheduled to be back
online in 3 weeks' time.

741
00:47:32,047 --> 00:47:33,465
On a personal note...

742
00:47:33,585 --> 00:47:36,457
It's good to have Lt. Uhura
back on duty.

743
00:47:37,341 --> 00:47:40,208
Well, Dr. Leonard McCoy.

744
00:47:40,328 --> 00:47:42,037
Surgeon to the gods.

745
00:47:42,157 --> 00:47:45,943
Who would have thought.
Just an old country doctor like me.

746
00:47:46,063 --> 00:47:47,273
You must be quite proud.

747
00:47:47,393 --> 00:47:49,351
Darn tootin'.
I may write a paper.

748
00:47:49,750 --> 00:47:53,926
I find it highly unlikely that Starfleet would
allow such a paper to be published.

749
00:47:55,915 --> 00:47:59,665
Based on the highly sensitive subject matter,
of course.

750
00:47:59,785 --> 00:48:01,394
Well regardless, gentlemen.

751
00:48:01,514 --> 00:48:05,224
Apollo learned what it took our respective
peoples centuries to discover.

752
00:48:05,603 --> 00:48:08,954
Self sacrifice is the best way to live.

753
00:48:09,074 --> 00:48:11,321
Benefitting both the giver and the receiver.

754
00:48:11,441 --> 00:48:14,594
If I had an entire culture worshipping
at my feet on bended knee...

755
00:48:14,606 --> 00:48:17,970
...I'm not so sure I'd be in a hurry
to change things either.

756
00:48:18,090 --> 00:48:21,374
Well, one thing's clear.
They sure did love humanity.

757
00:48:21,494 --> 00:48:23,408
Apollo still does.

758
00:48:23,528 --> 00:48:26,919
Just something about us lovable humans. 
Don't you think, Jim?

759
00:48:27,903 --> 00:48:30,483
Oh now. Don't pout Mr. Spock.

760
00:48:30,603 --> 00:48:34,658
I'm sure they would have loved the Vulcans
just as much had they landed there.

761
00:48:34,778 --> 00:48:36,812
What's not to love?

762
00:48:36,932 --> 00:48:38,955
I wasn't...

763
00:48:39,486 --> 00:48:41,827
Captain, I'll be at my station.

764
00:48:43,795 --> 00:48:46,827
Take us out of here, Mr. Sulu.
Ahead warp 2.

765
00:48:46,947 --> 00:48:47,784
Aye, sir.

766
00:49:43,073 --> 00:49:45,488
Thank you! Thank you! 

767
00:49:45,608 --> 00:49:48,016
You can't know what this means!

768
00:49:48,136 --> 00:49:52,489
You've done my family a great service!
Thank you! Thank you!

769
00:51:14,073 --> 00:51:16,073
English subtitles by:
Peter Goundrill

